      <div class="story">
      <p>
        I was very active back in the day, getting ~15 million views… Focus on <em>was</em>, for there’s nothing left of it.
        Only an empty YT, my Steam and MyAnimeList profiles, and my LinkedIn, which remains active.
      </p>
      <p>
        And as to why? Read my <a href="https://www.husbandoutworld.com/about/afterwords/#vol-4-afterword-2027">#HusbandOutWorld Vol 4 afterword</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="influencer-q">But wait… why build this website, then? Doesn’t that go against what you wrote in Vol 4?</p>
      <p>
        No, it doesn’t. I’m stepping back from the author/influencer persona in real life, down to removing all the stickers
        from my car and blending into the background. Meanwhile, I built this website to fulfill my “influencer” side on my own
        terms. If you’re here, it’s because you had to come find me; you didn’t stumble onto me passively. There’s no “like,”
        no “comment,” no engagement button of any kind. Not even an RSS feed, even though it would be easy to add.
      </p>
      <p>
        You’ve entered my virtual kingdom, so to speak, so it runs under my rules, including that I have no idea who came here
        or what they saw. The only metric I can see is whether someone goes out of their way to star or clone the GitHub repo.
        In a way, this is closer to the old web.
      </p>
      <p>
        This website fulfills my need to share the joy and growth, and it gives me something I can pull up on the fly to show
        the folks I’m talking with, without triggering hypervigilance. And just like the reason I went with self-publishing
        instead of traditional publishing: I can do whatever the fuck I want here, and nobody gives a shit…
      </p>
      <p>
        Will I ever come back? Likely not, because the ROI in knowledge and fun I was getting from being a social influencer
        towards the end of the journey was gone, and it had become hypervigilance and misery instead. I noticed I was being
        stalked too, so I cut it off at the root like an infection, healed, and moved on. I'm no longer trackable on the fly,
        and the daily push to engage and produce content is gone for good.
      </p>
      <p>For historical purposes, here’s a screenshot of what it looked like:</p>
